#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068593 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068755 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7ee9f8f9-37dd-5e7f-9a5c-b6d757c12025" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Rational Expressions With No Common Factor"}] :question/id #uuid "5d90ba01-2318-5f03-9d7a-a7ad66555886" :question/math-academy-id "e-5131" :question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac 2 x \\cdot \\dfrac 3 {y^2}$." :question/worked-solution "We multiply the numerators and the denominators as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac 2 x \\cdot \\dfrac 3 {y^2} &=\\dfrac {2 \\cdot 3} {x \\cdot y^2} &=\\dfrac {6} {xy^2} \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068594 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068756 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a771c493-d19b-561b-94e6-dc421b828b44" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Rational Expressions and Simplifying the Result"}] :question/id #uuid "702e4d33-7021-5b21-9c9d-d41463f7b665" :question/math-academy-id "e-5132" :question/problem "Given that $\\dfrac {6a} {b^2} = x$ and $\\dfrac {3b} {2a}= y$, what is $x\\cdot y$?" :question/worked-solution "We multiply the numerators and the denominators as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx \\cdot y &= \\frac{6a}{b^{2}} \\cdot \\frac{3b}{2a} \\\\\n&= \\frac{6a \\cdot 3b}{b^{2} \\cdot 2a} \\\\\n&= \\frac{18ab}{2ab^{2}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{9}{b}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Note:** In the above example, we performed some cancellations after multiplying. However, with more complex expressions, it's often easier to cancel *before* multiplying."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068595 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068757 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b2a63d1-c113-5866-8128-348fa0e59b22" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Rational Expressions by Canceling Common Terms"}] :question/id #uuid "e75ba094-e762-5e8d-9175-1d9617d32128" :question/math-academy-id "e-5133" :question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac {(n + 1)(n + 3)} {4n^2} \\cdot \\dfrac {8n}{(n + 3)(n - 2)}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we cancel any terms that appear in both a numerator and a denominator. Then, we multiply the numerators and the denominators as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n((n + 1)(n + 3))/(4n^{2}) \\cdot (8n)/((n + 3)(n - 2)) &= ((n + 1)(n + 3))/(4n \\cdot n) \\cdot (2 \\cdot 4n)/((n + 3)(n - 2)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{n + 1}{n} \\cdot \\frac{2}{n - 2} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2n + 2}{n^{2} - 2n}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068596 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068758 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2524afb3-e044-545e-8f7d-14a6ade93fb5" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Rational Expressions by Factoring and Canceling Common Terms"}] :question/id #uuid "e521a677-4e95-5c86-80c1-447924b6d34d" :question/math-academy-id "e-5134" :question/problem "Simplify $\\dfrac {3p - 12}{p^2 - 9} \\cdot \\dfrac {p + 3} {p - 4}$." :question/worked-solution "First, we factor all of the expressions and cancel any terms that appear in both a numerator and a denominator. Then, we multiply the numerators and the denominators as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\dfrac {3p - 12}{p^2 - 9} \\cdot \\dfrac {p + 3} {p - 4} &=\\dfrac {3(p - 4)} {(p + 3)(p - 3)} \\cdot \\dfrac {p + 3} {p - 4} \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac {3\\cancel{(p - 4)}} {\\cancel{(p + 3)}(p - 3)} \\cdot \\dfrac {\\cancel{p + 3}} {\\cancel{p - 4}} \\\\[5pt] &=\\dfrac {3}{p - 3} \\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
